# Unlock the LUKS /home from the first interactive login after a WSL instance
# start, then hand back a session indistinguishable from a normal one.
#
# WHY THIS LIVES IN /etc AND NOT IN chezmoi: everything chezmoi manages is on
# /home, which is exactly what is unreadable when this needs to run. A hook for
# the locked state cannot live inside the thing that is locked.
#
# WHY A SHELL HOOK AND NOT sshd/PAM: an sshd ForceCommand would also intercept
# `ssh fractal <cmd>`, scp and sftp, so every non-interactive caller (Ansible,
# agents, cc-clip) would have to be taught to step around it. A PAM hook would
# cover those too, but a wrong answer in the PAM stack costs SSH access to a
# machine whose console is a Windows terminal. This fires only where a human is
# already typing, and the worst failure is the shell you would have got anyway.
#
# The exec at the end is the point of the whole file. Unlocking mid-session
# leaves the shell holding a config, PATH and SSH_AUTH_SOCK it resolved while
# /home was missing; re-execing re-runs the whole startup sequence against the
# now-present home, which is what makes this equivalent to reconnecting.

if not status is-interactive
    return
end

# No TTY means no way to answer the passphrase prompt — cryptsetup would fail
# and take the login down with it.
if not isatty stdin
    return
end

# Only the operator's own login should be able to trigger a passphrase prompt.
if test (id -un) != {{ podhaus_operator }}
    return
end

if mountpoint -q /home
    return
end

# Survives the exec below, so a failed unlock degrades to an ordinary (if
# unconfigured) shell instead of re-entering this file forever. On the success
# path the mountpoint check above returns first and this never comes up.
if set -q __PODHAUS_HOME_UNLOCK_TRIED
    return
end
set -gx __PODHAUS_HOME_UNLOCK_TRIED 1

if not sudo unlock-home
    echo "home-unlock: /home is still locked; continuing with an unconfigured shell." >&2
    return
end

# The systemd user manager started while /home was locked, so its view of
# ~/.config/systemd/user is empty and stayed that way — nothing re-reads the
# unit search path on its own. Without this, machine-ssh-agent.service never
# starts, and the shell below comes up with no SSH_AUTH_SOCK.
#
# Both calls are advisory: a session without a user manager should still get
# its shell, so neither failure aborts the login.
systemctl --user daemon-reload 2>/dev/null
systemctl --user start default.target 2>/dev/null

exec fish -l
